her they could and I mean that the the  idea of venture philanthropy is is  exciting and it's important I think any  investing that we did in the space would  happen through the foundation and not  individually are you so involved or do  you feel like you're filling a void in  terms of philanthropy specifically in  medical research in part because NIH has  lost so much funding so there's no  question that there's been a huge  historical hit to research and  development the u.s. because NIH funding  has not been restored and has not  continued you know at the pace of  inflation over the last decade or so  there's a there's an organization I'm  involved with called act for NIH that's  working specifically on this issue and  there's an in fact a bill in front of  Congress called 21st century cures which  hopefully will restore NIH funding to  its previous levels and those are those  are both really important things but but  I think I think fundamentally cancer  research is extremely well-funded  there's a question about how effectively  are those funds being utilized I think  that's the more important question in  the case of of cancer research that was  market-makers anchors Stephanie ruhle  speaking with Sean Parker about the  launch of his foundation
 

